<%#
  T-37 — ONE EDITOR, ON THREE PAGES.

  `new`, `edit` and `preview` all render this. Before T-37 the first two carried their own
  copy of the form-plus-buttons and `preview` carried no editor at all, which is why
  §9b.2's clause — *"on failure it shows FR-58's diagnostics … **in the editor, next to
  the code**, which is the only place it is actionable"* — was not met: the diagnostics
  were on a page with no code on it, and the way back to the editor was the browser's Back
  button, which loses the render you just looked at.

  --- WHAT THE THREE PAGES DIFFER IN, AND IT IS ONLY THIS ---

    url / method     where Save goes: POST to the collection, or PATCH to the record
    preview_url      where the Preview button goes, which needs the id when there is one
    submit_label     Create or Save

  Everything else — the fields, the buttons, the lint panel and their order — is here, so
  the three surfaces cannot come to disagree about which controls an author has.

  --- THE PREVIEW BUTTON'S TWO ATTRIBUTES ARE LOAD-BEARING ---

  `formaction` sends this form's own body (the UNSAVED content) to the preview action, and
  `formmethod: 'post'` changes the verb without changing the body — which still carries
  Rails' hidden `_method=patch` on the edit and preview pages, so `Rack::MethodOverride`
  rewrites the request to PATCH before routing. Both verbs are declared on the preview
  route for exactly that reason; getting it wrong is a 404 on every press, which is what
  the first version of T-23's editor did.
%>
<%# T-37 — the chart form's script, and it is the ONLY JavaScript the authoring chrome loads.
    The editor itself is a plain `<textarea>` and needs none: `[OQ-M]` closed the CodeMirror
    path, so there is no enhanced editor to fall back FROM. This script reveals its own
    fieldset, which is why the feature is absent rather than broken when it does not run. %>
<% content_for :header_tags do %>
  <%= javascript_include_tag 'chart_form', plugin: 'redmine_reporter_dashboards' %>
<% end %>

<%= labelled_form_for :template, @template,
                      url: url,
                      html: { method: method, id: 'reporter-template-form' } do |f| %>
  <%= render partial: 'form', locals: { f: f } %>
  <p>
    <%= submit_tag submit_label %>
    <%= submit_tag l(:label_preview),
                   name: 'preview',
                   formaction: preview_url,
                   formmethod: 'post' %>
    <%= link_to l(:button_cancel), project_reporter_templates_path(@project) %>
  </p>
<% end %>

<%# OUTSIDE THE FORM, DELIBERATELY. It is a report about the body, not a control that
    submits with it, and nesting it inside would put a `<table>` in a form for no reason.
    `@lint` is set by every action that renders this partial — see the controller's
    `lint_editor`, which is the one place it is computed. %>
<%= render partial: 'lint', locals: { analysis: @lint } %>

<%# FR-73's second half — a form that INSERTS the tag and stores nothing. Below the lint
    panel rather than above it: what the template does wrong is more urgent than a
    convenience for adding to it. %>
<%= render partial: 'chart_form' %>

<%# FR-72's reference, in Redmine's own sidebar slot rather than in a box of our own.
    `content_for` is used here — in the shared partial — for the same reason the buttons are:
    three pages would otherwise each have to remember it, and the one that forgot would be
    the one where an author is looking something up. %>
<% content_for :sidebar do %>
  <%= render partial: 'drop_reference' %>
<% end %>
